;; expand-hand-extent: a `','`, `'<'` or `'>'` character literal in blaze-expand's sources outside
;; extent.rs. That is how a hand walk finds where a field, variant, generic parameter, argument
;; or meta ends: by counting commas outside groups, or `<` and `>` as if they were delimiters. No
;; Rust element is delimited so (`HashMap<K, V>`, `f::<A, B>()`, `|a, b| a` and `T = HashMap<K, V>`
;; each hold a comma and end no element there). Two such walks were today's two bugs:
;; item.rs `element_end` on reth's `ChainHardforks` (t1545) and builtin.rs `cfg_select!` on a
;; turbofish (t1800). Every extent comes from crates/blaze-expand/src/extent.rs, the parser's
;; reading (end-state.md D167(d): one walk per syntax).
;;
;; A site that matches one of these characters for another reason says why on an
;; `astlog-ignore: expand-hand-extent` comment: the macro language's own separators
;; (`$(..),*`), a token the expansion writes, or a walk not yet moved onto extent.rs, named as
;; LEFT.
(rule (expand-hand-extent c)
  (match rust "(char_literal) @c")
  (text-match c "^'[,<>]'$")
  (file-path c p)
  (text-match p "(^|/)crates/blaze-expand/src/")
  (not (text-match p "/extent[.]rs$")))

(lint expand-hand-extent error
  "a `,`, `<` or `>` matched in blaze-expand outside extent.rs: an element's extent is the parser's (crate::extent::Words::extent, Words::list, extent::item); count no commas or angles by hand")
